    {
      "abstract": "As commercial interest in floating offshore wind turbine (FOWT) projects increases, research into mooring system design for these devices has become prevalent in academia. While some research into FOWT mooring system optimisation has been published, this work does not account for the cost of the anchors, which must be sized according to the mooring system loads and form a substantial part of the FOWT system cost. This thesis seeks to reconcile anchor and mooring design for FOWT projects through the development of an optimisation tool for minimising the total cost of both the anchors and mooring system. The Anchor Optimisation Design Tool (AODT), created for and detailed in this work, uses numerical modelling and a genetic algorithm method to determine the optimal geometry and cost for a groutless rock anchor in a given design load and set of geotechnical conditions. These groutless rock anchors used the Swift Anchor design by SCHOTTEL Marine Technologies (SMT), and were optimised to find the lowest total manufacturing and installation cost. In developing the installation cost model for the AODT, a new numerical model for predicting Swift Anchor drilling times was derived, validated and presented in this thesis. The Mooring Optimisation Design Tool (MODT) was developed to find the mooring design parameters with the lowest total anchor and mooring system cost for a given FOWT platform and environment. The MODT also used a genetic algorithm optimisation method, and evaluated potential mooring configurations using a combination of dynamic simulations in OrcaFlex and interpolation using the kriging method. The MODT accessed data generated by the AODT to predict the anchor cost of each mooring system under evaluation, based on the anchor loads observed. The MODT was used to optimise a nylon rope mooring system for a 15 MW FOWT on a semi-submersible platform in 8 case studies. The principles of the AODT were applied to create an equivalent anchor cost model representing traditional drilled and grouted piles. Mooring optimisations were run for both anchor types, so to observe the affects of anchor type on the optimal system design and cost. The case studies show using Swift Anchors over traditional drilled and grouted rock anchor technology allows a 45.0% to 55.5% cost reduction of the total anchor and mooring system, depending on environmental factors. The method of combined anchor and mooring system optimisation detailed in this thesis presents a significant step forward in optimisation of balance of plant systems for FOWT, and has potential for finding substantial cost savings in future FOWT projects.",
